  "example": "The model calls `list_files` to find a directory, then `ripgrep` to find matching\nlines in a Rust file. The listing creates no evidence input; the search returns\nand records file-range inputs including five context lines on each side, where\navailable. A `habeas` request with that file's `rust_file` and\na `rust_item` selector returns and records the complete Rust item, along with any\nrequired terminology. A file-range request omitting `line_end` reads through EOF\nand records the actual ending line without requiring the model to know it.\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.file_discovery`\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.text_search`\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.source_tools`\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.delivered_inputs`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests cover each step of the `additional_read` scenario, and three tests carry `{test}` references to it. I judged this from the supplied test sources and did not read the implementation.\n\n**Coverage by step**\n\n- **`list_files` creates no evidence.** In `src/authoring/tools.rs`, `reading_records_only_delivered_sources_and_their_terms` lists `src` and checks the entry is `src/lib.rs` with kind `file`. It also asserts `listing.inputs.is_empty()`.\n- **`ripgrep` returns file-range inputs with five lines of context per side, clamped at file boundaries.** `search_merges_context_windows_and_respects_ignore_rules` (same file) searches a 20-line file with matches on lines 2 and 6. It expects one merged input covering lines 1–11 with the exact text. Line 2 minus five clamps to line 1, and line 6 plus five gives line 11. The same test covers related search behaviour:\n  - `context: 0` returns only the matching lines.\n  - A search with no matches returns empty arrays.\n  - A bad regex or a negative context returns an error.\n  - Ignored and hidden files are respected.\n- **A `rust_file` + `rust_item` read returns the whole item plus required terminology.** The first test reads `fn[work]` from `src/lib.rs`, which carries a `term` role. It asserts that two inputs come back and that `definitions[0].definition` is `terms.value`.\n- **Only delivered inputs are recorded, without duplicates.** The first test asserts that nothing is recorded before `record_inputs`. It calls `record_inputs` twice and asserts the recorded count is 2.\n- **A file-range read without `line_end` reads through EOF and reports the real end line.** The first test requests `{file, line_start: 2}` and asserts `line_end == 2` and the expected text. It also rejects `line_start: 0`.\n- **Request forms are validated.** The same test rejects incomplete, mixed and unknown request forms, and accepts the valid `requirements` and `definition` forms.\n\n**End-to-end check.** `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` in `tests/authoring_cli.rs` runs a model session that calls `list_files`, `ripgrep` and `habeas` (including a repeated read and a failed read). It then inspects the written evidence:\n- `extra.txt` appears with search windows of lines 1–11 and a read window of lines 10–12, and the repeated read is deduplicated.\n- The mandatory definition input is present.\n- The listing, the failed `absent.txt` read and the `.` path leave no evidence inputs.\n\nI found no gaps and no FIXME markers, so the verdict is true.",
